    "new": "fit one hand of menthol far better than the other.</p>\n</div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Skeletal structures of menthol and carvone with their chiral carbons marked by red asterisks. Left, menthol: a cyclohexane ring carrying an OH group, an isopropyl group on the neighboring ring carbon, and a methyl group on the ring carbon two positions away from the OH carbon on the other side; the three ring carbons that hold the OH, the isopropyl group and the methyl group are starred, so menthol has 3 chiral carbons. Right, carvone: a six-membered ring containing a ketone C=O and a C=C double bond, with a methyl group on the double-bond carbon next to the C=O and an isopropenyl group, C(CH3)=CH2, on the ring; only the ring carbon holding the isopropenyl group is starred, so carvone has 1 chiral carbon.\" width=\"330\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/{{k2chir}}/fig-p597106-menthol-carvone.svg\" fileid=\"{{k2chir}}\" /><figcaption>Figure 5.5.9: Chiral carbons (*) in two molecules you can smell. Menthol has three chiral carbons, so 2<sup>3</sup> = 8 stereoisomers are possible; carvone has one, so it exists as a pair of enantiomers (one smells of spearmint, the other of caraway). (Structures drawn by Daniel Kwasniewski, CC BY 4.0.)</figcaption></figure>",
